meta {
  name: 7 Cancel inside the 24-hour window
  type: http
  seq: 7
}
delete {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/booking/appointments/
  body: json
}
body:json {
  {
    "identifier": "{{appointmentIdentifier}}",
    "emailProvider": "{{providerEmail}}",
    "emailCustomer": "{{customerEmail}}"
  }
}
docs {
  The same route as #3 — this entry exists to exercise the **notice period**, which is asymmetric.

  A **CUSTOMER** must give 24 hours' notice. Inside that window this returns `400` with the deadline
  named as a concrete local time:

      "Cancellations close 24 hours before a session. This one closed on Monday 7 September at
       2:00 AM. Contact the provider to ask."

  A **PROVIDER** may cancel at any notice and gets `204`. A session they cannot make is unavoidable; a
  customer cancelling an hour beforehand costs the provider a slot nobody else can now take.

  ⚠️ **The rule lives in the same atomic filter as the cancellable-status rule**, not in a check before
  it. Reading the appointment, comparing its start against the clock and then writing is a race a
  caller can win — the read and the write are separate operations.

  To see the refusal: book a session less than 24 hours out, sign in as the customer, and run this.
  The client hides its Cancel button in that window and shows the deadline instead, so this refusal is
  reachable through the API but not through the app.
}
